%U A398223 35,11,8,21,17,11,44,15,15,33,9,12,15,23,19,41,8,8,30,30
%N A398223 a(n) is the number of iterations for the map f(k) = k/3 if k == 0 (mod 3), 4k+1 if k == 1 (mod 3), 2k-1 if k == 2 (mod 3), to first reach a previously-visited value, starting from k=n.
%C A398223 This is a three-branch analog of the Collatz (3x+1) map, branching on residue mod 3 instead of mod 2. Empirically (not proved), every trajectory eventually reaches one of exactly two cycles: (1, 3, 5, 9) and (7, 11, 17, 19, 21, 29, 33, 51, 57, 77, 153). It is conjectured, but not proved, that no starting value diverges to infinity. Verified by direct computation for all n from 1 to 10^6 with no divergences and no cycles other than the two listed. Some trajectories transiently reach very large intermediate values (on the order of 10^15 or more) before collapsing into a cycle.
%F A398223 a(n) = (least k > 0 such that f^k(n) = f^j(n) for some 0 <= j < k), where f(m) = m/3 if m == 0 (mod 3), f(m) = 4m+1 if m == 1 (mod 3), f(m) = 2m-1 if m == 2 (mod 3). Empirical, not proved to be well-defined for all n (see comments).
%e A398223 For n = 4: trajectory is 4, 17, 50, ... eventually repeating after a(4) = 12 steps.
%e A398223 For n = 7: trajectory is 7, 29, 15, 5, 9, 1, 3, 5, ... repeating after a(7) = 11 steps (7 itself is part of cycle C2, but the count is to the first repeated value encountered along the way).
%o A398223 (Python)
%o A398223 from itertools import count
%o A398223 def f(n):
%o A398223     if n % 3 == 0: return n // 3
%o A398223     if n % 3 == 1: return 4*n + 1
%o A398223     return 2*n - 1
%o A398223 def a(n):
%o A398223     seen = {n: 0}
%o A398223     cur = n
%o A398223     for step in count(1):
%o A398223         cur = f(cur)
%o A398223         if cur in seen: return step
%o A398223         seen[cur] = step
%Y A398223 Cf. A006577, A398157.
%K A398223 nonn,new
%O A398223 1,1
